# Normalise line endings in the repository.
#
# Git's `core.autocrlf` default differs per machine and per install, so without this the
# same file is stored differently depending on who committed it. The visible symptom on
# Windows is a "LF will be replaced by CRLF" warning on every `git add`, and the subtler
# one is that a one-character edit can show up in a diff as the whole file changing.
#
# `text=auto` lets Git detect text files and store them with LF, converting to the local
# convention on checkout. `eol=lf` pins the working tree to LF as well, so the bytes match
# what the repository holds and the warning stops.
* text=auto eol=lf

# PNG and ICO are binary — never touch their bytes.
*.png binary
*.ico binary

# Keep these exactly as generated; a line-ending rewrite would make lockfile diffs noise.
Cargo.lock -text
package-lock.json -text

# Batch and PowerShell files are the one place Windows genuinely needs CRLF, because
# cmd.exe and some PowerShell hosts mis-handle LF in scripts.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
